  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 113 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nDry northwest flow will dominate the weather from Wednesday \nthrough the end of the workweek. This will keep skies mostly \nclear, and allow for warming afternoon temperatures. Highs will be\nin the mid 70s to lower 80s. While afternoon temperatures will be\nwarmer, the dry air and clear skies will allow for overnight lows\nto drop into the 40s to lower 50s. \n\nBy Friday afternoon, the next upper level low to impact the area \nwill be moving east towards northwestern Mexico. As pressure falls\noccur ahead of this system to our west, our surface and low level\nwinds will increase out of the south and southeast, advecting \nGulf moisture into the area. Large-scale lift from the upper level\ntrough, combined with upslope flow and southerly warm air \nadvection should allow for a low to medium (20%-50%) chance for \nshowers and possibly a few thunderstorms to develop over the \nweekend. The most likely timeframe at this point will be from \nSaturday afternoon into Saturday night. As mentioned previously, \nSaturday is expected to be quite a bit cooler than the second half\nof this week with increased cloud cover, an upper level low \nmoving through, and the chance for precipitation. Depending on how\nfast the trough moves east of the area Sunday, we may see some \nclearing skies during the afternoon. A cold front behind the \nsystem will bring northeasterly flow with cooler temperatures and \ndry weather early next week.",
